<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
    <title>Design preview</title>
    <%# The compiled component stylesheet, plus editor_canvas.css -- never
        stylesheet_link_tag "tabler_ui/docs" (docs.css), which styles editor
        chrome (toolbar, rails, tabs, ...) the design being previewed has no
        business inheriting. This document shows the design, not the editor
        around it -- see EditorController#frame. editor_canvas.css is the
        deliberate exception to that rule: it styles nothing but the
        selection/hover overlay editor/overlay.js draws over the design (see
        that file's and editor_canvas.css's own headers for why the overlay
        is drawn here, in this document, rather than in the parent page --
        getBoundingClientRect() coordinates only line up with a position:
        fixed overlay when both live in the same document), and every one of
        its selectors is scoped to a class only that overlay's own markup
        carries, so the design itself is never touched by it. %>
    <%= stylesheet_link_tag "tabler_ui_all" %>
    <%= stylesheet_link_tag "tabler_ui/docs/editor_canvas" %>
    <%# Same importmap-rails guard as the main docs layout (application.html.erb)
        -- a host without importmap-rails installed (including this gem's
        own Combustion spec harness) gets zero script tags here too. %>
    <% if respond_to?(:javascript_importmap_tags) %>
      <%= javascript_importmap_tags %>
    <% end %>
  </head>
  <body>
    <%# The editor's own JS writes the rendered design into this element --
        see EditorController#preview's response shape (html:) and this
        layout's own doc comment. The child template (editor/frame.html.erb)
        contributes nothing of its own; yield is only here so this stays an
        ordinary Rails layout rather than a special case. %>
    <div id="tabler-ui-editor-canvas"><%= yield %></div>
  </body>
</html>
